  "notes": "TRANSITIVITY:\nIntransitive. No standard transitive counterpart.\n\nIchidan verb ({一段動詞|いちだんどうし}).\n- Negative: {寂|さび}れない\n- Polite: {寂|さび}れます\n- Te-form: {寂|さび}れて\n- Past: {寂|さび}れた\n- Participle (adjective-like): {寂|さび}れた ({町|まち} — deserted town)\n\nUSAGE:\nThe past participle {寂|さび}れた is frequently used as a modifier: {寂|さび}れた{町|まち} (a deserted town), {寂|さび}れた{商店街|しょうてんがい} (a run-down shopping street). Closely associated with rural depopulation and the decline of local economies in Japan.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {寂|さび}れた{町|まち}: a deserted/declining town\n- {寂|さび}れた{商店街|しょうてんがい}: a run-down shopping street\n- {寂|さび}れていく: to gradually decline\n- {寂|さび}れた{温泉|おんせん}: a once-popular hot spring that has declined",
